1
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:08,791
Did you know that the sea air
is a natural antihistamine?

2
00:00:08,878 --> 00:00:12,621
The salt clears the passages.

3
00:00:12,708 --> 00:00:14,449
- Hey, Cho.
- What do we got?

4
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:16,842
Alton Creek. He's a big-time
divorce attorney.

5
00:00:16,929 --> 00:00:19,367
It's hard to say which one
of his many enemies

6
00:00:19,454 --> 00:00:20,542
would want him dead.

7
00:00:20,629 --> 00:00:21,847
His speedboat's down there.

8
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:23,110
Cause of death?

9
00:00:23,197 --> 00:00:24,763
Well, he's not
dead yet.

10
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:26,809
Somebody just threatened
to make him that way.

11
00:00:26,896 --> 00:00:29,116
Threatened? Since when do
we roll for death threats?

12
00:00:29,203 --> 00:00:32,162
When the threatened man
is a rich establishment lawyer

13
00:00:32,249 --> 00:00:33,250
with juice.

14
00:00:33,337 --> 00:00:34,730
This one's on you, Cho.

15
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:36,036
Apparently one of your

16
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:38,212
confidential informants
knows reek.

17
00:00:38,299 --> 00:00:39,865
She told him we could help.

18
00:00:39,952 --> 00:00:41,041
- Summer.
- Uh-huh.

19
00:00:41,128 --> 00:00:42,564
Summer?
That's a nice name.

20
00:00:42,651 --> 00:00:45,654
She's a working girl.
Been a C.I. for a few weeks.

21
00:00:45,741 --> 00:00:47,656
Surprised she knows
a high-end guy like Creek.

22
00:00:47,743 --> 00:00:49,484
She's moving up.

23
00:00:49,571 --> 00:00:51,181
[Creek]
'Where are you?'

24
00:00:51,268 --> 00:00:53,792
Hi. Yeah. Uh, Mr. Creek,
w-we're walking to you now.

25
00:00:53,879 --> 00:00:55,446
[Creek]
'You're late.'

26
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:57,100
It's a long drive
from Sacramento.

27
00:00:57,187 --> 00:00:59,233
We got here as soon
as we could.

28
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,496
That's it? Moron, someone's
trying to kill me.

29
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:04,064
And the CBI needs to do
something about these threats

30
00:01:04,151 --> 00:01:06,588
or I will call my good friend,
the attorney general..

31
00:01:06,675 --> 00:01:08,590
[explosion]

32
00:01:20,254 --> 00:01:23,126
[theme music]

33
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:32,918
[crackling and whooshing]

34
00:01:46,845 --> 00:01:48,325
- Uh..
- His leg?

35
00:01:49,674 --> 00:01:51,763
Was that a shark?

36
00:01:55,463 --> 00:01:57,639
So much for
professional courtesy.

37
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:04,254
[Rigsby]
So forensics finished
their search of the water

38
00:02:04,341 --> 00:02:05,734
near the explosion
and confirmed

39
00:02:05,821 --> 00:02:07,779
that the body parts belonged
to Alton Creek.

40
00:02:07,866 --> 00:02:09,477
Did they... find them all?

41
00:02:09,564 --> 00:02:13,263
Most. And the arson squad found
traces of c-4 in the debris.

42
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:14,830
Guy never knew
what hit him.

43
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:16,875
At least he was dead
when, you know..

44
00:02:16,962 --> 00:02:18,355
...the shark ate him.

45
00:02:20,488 --> 00:02:22,098
Okay, um, anything else?

46
00:02:22,185 --> 00:02:23,708
Uh, time frame.

47
00:02:23,795 --> 00:02:25,623
Security cameras covering
the yacht's berth

48
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:28,757
were down for repairs between 11
last night and 7 this morning.

49
00:02:28,844 --> 00:02:30,802
Killer had to have planted
the explosive then.

50
00:02:30,889 --> 00:02:32,935
- Is that luck or planning?
- Don't know yet.

51
00:02:33,022 --> 00:02:34,589
So who wanted this guy dead?

52
00:02:34,676 --> 00:02:36,156
He's a divorce attorney.
Who didn't?

53
00:02:36,243 --> 00:02:37,983
Does he have any family?

54
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:41,552
Nope. We're trying to find his
law partner, Lawrence Dersh.

55
00:02:41,639 --> 00:02:43,685
He's not at any
of his three houses.

56
00:02:43,772 --> 00:02:45,556
[woman speaks indistinctly]

57
00:02:45,643 --> 00:02:48,429
Ah.

58
00:02:48,516 --> 00:02:50,735
Maybe Summer'll know.
I asked her to come in.

59
00:02:50,822 --> 00:02:53,999
Kimball Cho.
It's been a while.

60
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:56,959
You're late.

61
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,788
You know sharks can have up
to 3000 teeth?

62
00:02:59,875 --> 00:03:01,224
Five rows of 'em.

63
00:03:01,311 --> 00:03:03,487
Are you gonna actually
help on the case?

64
00:03:03,574 --> 00:03:06,490
'I could actually use
your help, too.'

65
00:03:06,577 --> 00:03:08,971
Special agent Darcy.

66
00:03:09,058 --> 00:03:11,495
How's the federal authorities?
I haven't seen you for, uh..

67
00:03:11,582 --> 00:03:13,497
Since, what..

68
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:15,760
The Panzer business, right.
Actually, that's why I'm here.

69
00:03:15,847 --> 00:03:17,327
We just took over the case.

70
00:03:17,414 --> 00:03:19,373
SFPD wasn't getting
anywhere.

71
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,853
Can I ask you
a couple of questions?

72
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,681
- Um, we're kind of--
- 'Oh, of course.'

73
00:03:23,768 --> 00:03:25,988
Uh, whatever you need.
Take a seat.

74
00:03:26,075 --> 00:03:27,555
I'll, I'll make tea.

75
00:03:30,253 --> 00:03:33,561
Is this an official inquiry?
Cop to confidential informant?

76
00:03:33,648 --> 00:03:35,302
You'll get paid.

77
00:03:35,389 --> 00:03:37,913
Now did Creek tell you
who wanted to kill him?

78
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,132
Wasn't that kind
of relationship.

79
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,612
How's your back?

80
00:03:42,744 --> 00:03:44,311
It's fine.

81
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:46,095
Whatwas your relationship?

82
00:03:46,182 --> 00:03:47,705
He used to be a client.

83
00:03:47,792 --> 00:03:49,707
We weren't close,
but I liked him.

84
00:03:49,794 --> 00:03:52,101
Well, you seem to be
one of the few.

85
00:03:52,188 --> 00:03:56,584
Yeah, well,
Alton was...abrasive.

86
00:03:56,671 --> 00:03:58,760
He was a real lawyer,
you know?

87
00:03:58,847 --> 00:04:02,154
Wasn't really happy unless
he was arguing about something.

88
00:04:02,242 --> 00:04:04,853
- Girlfriend?
- New one every month.

89
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:06,681
You know his law partner,
Lawrence Dersh?

90
00:04:06,768 --> 00:04:09,031
No, not really.
But you know what?

91
00:04:09,118 --> 00:04:10,946
Al was bitching
about Dersh last week

92
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:13,514
said they got in a fight
over money or something.

93
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:15,516
Could Dersh be the one
who threatened Creek?

94
00:04:15,603 --> 00:04:17,692
Maybe. I mean,
it was pretty hard-core.

95
00:04:17,779 --> 00:04:21,826
- You heard the threat?
- No. I saw it.

96
00:04:21,913 --> 00:04:23,611
We were coming back
from this party

97
00:04:23,698 --> 00:04:26,396
and it was sitting
on his front doorstep.

98
00:04:26,483 --> 00:04:28,964
A pig with a big old knife
in his head.

99
00:04:29,051 --> 00:04:32,054
I told him to go to the cops,
but he said no.

100
00:04:32,141 --> 00:04:36,188
Couple days later, he asked if
I knew any cops who were cool.

101
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:37,799
I couldn't think of any

102
00:04:37,886 --> 00:04:40,280
so I told him
to talk to you guys.

103
00:04:43,326 --> 00:04:45,807
Look, Red John was
an accomplished killer

104
00:04:45,894 --> 00:04:49,463
no doubt, and I applaud you
for ridding us of him.

105
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:51,639
But he is not
in the same league

106
00:04:51,726 --> 00:04:53,771
as the San Joaquin killer.

107
00:04:53,858 --> 00:04:56,296
'Red John was
a common sociopath..'

108
00:04:56,383 --> 00:04:58,515
...lazy, sloppy, delusional.

109
00:04:58,602 --> 00:05:01,388
'You really have no idea
what you're saying.'

110
00:05:01,475 --> 00:05:04,086
And only
a little while later..

111
00:05:04,173 --> 00:05:06,871
[tea pouring]

112
00:05:06,958 --> 00:05:09,091
Hmm.

113
00:05:09,178 --> 00:05:12,224
The man who killed Panzer wanted
it to look like Red John

114
00:05:12,312 --> 00:05:14,096
'whom you shot and killed
last spring.'

115
00:05:14,183 --> 00:05:15,445
Lapsang souchong.

116
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:17,012
I think you're gonna like it.

117
00:05:17,099 --> 00:05:19,754
Here we go.

118
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:21,843
Your point?

119
00:05:21,930 --> 00:05:24,149
Well, it's an odd chain
of events, isn't it?

120
00:05:24,236 --> 00:05:25,716
Mm.

121
00:05:25,803 --> 00:05:27,239
[slurps]

122
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:30,765
Yeah, I guess. Odd happens.

123
00:05:30,852 --> 00:05:32,636
What does it matter
who killed Panzer?

124
00:05:32,723 --> 00:05:34,508
He was the San Joaquin killer.

125
00:05:34,595 --> 00:05:37,511
So you said, but I studied
Panzer very carefully.

126
00:05:37,598 --> 00:05:39,774
There's not a lot of evidence
to support that.

127
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:41,253
Not your kind of evidence.

128
00:05:41,341 --> 00:05:42,994
He was the San Joaquin killer.

129
00:05:43,081 --> 00:05:44,779
I agree.

130
00:05:44,866 --> 00:05:50,001
It is possible, but his murderer
still has to be apprehended.

131
00:05:50,088 --> 00:05:52,526
Why?

132
00:05:52,613 --> 00:05:53,918
Because killing someone

133
00:05:54,005 --> 00:05:56,094
without government
permission is wrong?

134
00:05:56,181 --> 00:05:58,575
Was there something that
you wanted to ask, agent?

135
00:05:58,662 --> 00:06:01,752
Are you sure the man
you killed was Red John?

136
00:06:01,839 --> 00:06:03,624
[swallows]

137
00:06:07,018 --> 00:06:09,369
Yes. I'm sure.

138
00:06:14,809 --> 00:06:17,115
[clears throat]
Then we have a copycat.

139
00:06:19,291 --> 00:06:21,772
can you think of anyone
who would wanna do this?

140
00:06:21,859 --> 00:06:25,297
Someone that wanted to avenge
Panzer's crimes, I'd imagine.

141
00:06:25,385 --> 00:06:28,126
Well, who else thought that
he was the San Joaquin killer

142
00:06:28,213 --> 00:06:29,650
aside from you?

143
00:06:29,737 --> 00:06:31,086
Good question.

144
00:06:31,173 --> 00:06:32,914
You figure out
the answer to that

145
00:06:33,001 --> 00:06:34,698
I think you'll probably
have your killer.

146
00:06:34,785 --> 00:06:36,874
But surely the motive

147
00:06:36,961 --> 00:06:39,834
is to avenge the insult
to Red John, right?

148
00:06:39,921 --> 00:06:43,359
I mean, look at the...
the cutting patterns.

149
00:06:43,446 --> 00:06:44,882
Clearly, our suspect

150
00:06:44,969 --> 00:06:47,058
is someone who studied
Red John obsessively.

151
00:06:47,145 --> 00:06:50,497
Well, R-Red John..

152
00:06:50,584 --> 00:06:54,979
...he was the type to attract
followers, um, a-admirers.

153
00:06:55,066 --> 00:06:56,720
He had a particular style..

154
00:06:56,807 --> 00:06:59,201
...a style that people wanted
to, to copy, you know?

155
00:06:59,288 --> 00:07:01,943
'Not unlike Elvis.'

156
00:07:02,030 --> 00:07:03,335
Alright.

157
00:07:03,423 --> 00:07:05,599
Well, thank you
for your time.

158
00:07:05,686 --> 00:07:08,079
I'm staying
at the Oakview Inn.

159
00:07:08,166 --> 00:07:09,864
If you can think
of anything else

160
00:07:09,951 --> 00:07:12,388
please give me a call.

161
00:07:12,475 --> 00:07:14,129
Agent Lisbon.

162
00:07:18,655 --> 00:07:19,656
Nice lady.

163
00:07:19,743 --> 00:07:22,137
Nice?

164
00:07:22,224 --> 00:07:24,531
That's all you're gonna say?
She's nice?

165
00:07:24,618 --> 00:07:27,838
Great legs.

166
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:29,971
[indistinct chatter]

167
00:07:30,058 --> 00:07:33,148
Don't you think it's time to
tell the truth about Red John?

168
00:07:44,638 --> 00:07:46,814
[door closes]

169
00:07:46,901 --> 00:07:48,859
Well, the truth is
I killed Red John.

170
00:07:48,946 --> 00:07:52,036
I mean, you can ask anyone.

171
00:07:52,123 --> 00:07:53,951
It was on the news, Lisbon.

172
00:07:54,038 --> 00:07:57,346
But you think he's alive.

173
00:07:57,433 --> 00:07:59,740
I toldyou that.

174
00:07:59,827 --> 00:08:02,438
You, Lisbon. Nobody else.

175
00:08:02,525 --> 00:08:03,874
And it's better that way.

176
00:08:03,961 --> 00:08:05,528
Then why make him
re-emerge?

177
00:08:05,615 --> 00:08:08,139
You manipulated Panzer
into insulting Red John

178
00:08:08,226 --> 00:08:10,098
so Red John would kill him.

179
00:08:13,188 --> 00:08:15,625
I didn't know how else
to stop Panzer.

180
00:08:15,712 --> 00:08:19,934
He would have just
kept on killing.

181
00:08:20,021 --> 00:08:22,458
And now you've got the FBI
asking awkward questions.

182
00:08:22,545 --> 00:08:24,765
You've kept this from the team.
It's not good.

183
00:08:24,852 --> 00:08:27,028
It's lucky they don't know,
'cause if they knew

184
00:08:27,115 --> 00:08:28,856
they'd have to lie,
wouldn't they?

185
00:08:28,943 --> 00:08:31,902
And this way, they can tell
what they think is the truth.

186
00:08:31,989 --> 00:08:34,035
Do you know how messed up
that sounds?

187
00:08:34,122 --> 00:08:35,558
Yeah.

188
00:08:35,645 --> 00:08:37,734
What is wrong
with the simple truth?

189
00:08:37,821 --> 00:08:39,649
Telling people he's alive?

190
00:08:39,736 --> 00:08:42,173
I told a jury
that I shot him dead.

191
00:08:43,914 --> 00:08:45,481
Where does that leave me?

192
00:08:48,005 --> 00:08:49,616
It's not good.

193
00:08:49,703 --> 00:08:52,009
No, no, not great.

194
00:08:55,883 --> 00:08:58,015
Their partner dies,
and they throw a party.

195
00:08:58,102 --> 00:09:00,670
Nice.

196
00:09:00,757 --> 00:09:03,151
Hey, so you're stranded
on a desert island

197
00:09:03,238 --> 00:09:05,632
with Attila the Hun,
Joseph Stalin, and a lawyer

198
00:09:05,719 --> 00:09:08,939
and you got a gun, with only
two bullets. What do you do?

199
00:09:09,026 --> 00:09:10,550
- You're telling a lawyer joke?
- Yeah.

200
00:09:10,637 --> 00:09:11,681
No.

201
00:09:17,339 --> 00:09:20,081
Lawrence Dersh.
You're a tough man to find.

202
00:09:20,168 --> 00:09:24,564
You're here about Alton.
Awful thing. Just awful.

203
00:09:24,651 --> 00:09:27,697
But business
is business, right?

204
00:09:27,784 --> 00:09:30,004
This is our
yearly retreat, officers.

205
00:09:30,091 --> 00:09:31,919
It's been scheduled for months.

206
00:09:32,006 --> 00:09:34,617
The employees are scared.
I have to show them that

207
00:09:34,704 --> 00:09:36,880
business is still strong
even with Alton gone.

208
00:09:36,967 --> 00:09:38,534
He'd want that, I promise you.

209
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:41,624
Sir, where were you between
11 P.M. and 7 A.M.?

210
00:09:41,711 --> 00:09:43,887
Um, whoa. Sorry.
Flag on the play.

211
00:09:43,974 --> 00:09:46,411
My client won't be answering
any questions right now.

212
00:09:46,498 --> 00:09:47,891
Your client?

213
00:09:47,978 --> 00:09:51,025
Colette Santori. I work
with Larry at the firm.

214
00:09:51,112 --> 00:09:53,331
White-collar crime.

215
00:09:53,418 --> 00:09:55,246
Fast track to partnership,
right, Colette?

216
00:09:55,333 --> 00:09:57,248
Yeah, if you play
your cards right.

217
00:09:57,335 --> 00:10:02,079
No, uh, in-house dating,
Larry. Rules.

218
00:10:02,166 --> 00:10:04,778
Well, as you can see,
Larry's had a few

219
00:10:04,865 --> 00:10:06,693
and is saying
some inappropriate things.

220
00:10:06,780 --> 00:10:08,999
Not a good time to talk
to the police.

221
00:10:09,086 --> 00:10:10,784
Hey, now.

222
00:10:10,871 --> 00:10:14,135
But I would be happy to set up
an interview for tomorrow.

223
00:10:14,222 --> 00:10:16,485
Sure. No problem.
Just one thing.

224
00:10:16,572 --> 00:10:18,487
Who had reason
to want your partner dead?

225
00:10:18,574 --> 00:10:20,489
I have no idea.

226
00:10:20,576 --> 00:10:22,056
Like I said..

227
00:10:22,143 --> 00:10:24,319
'...tomorrow.'

228
00:10:24,406 --> 00:10:25,799
Thanks for your time.

229
00:10:40,248 --> 00:10:42,163
[computer chimes]

230
00:10:42,250 --> 00:10:44,295
What the hell?

231
00:10:44,382 --> 00:10:45,949
That's weird.

232
00:10:51,041 --> 00:10:53,304
'It's late, Grace.
What are you still doing here?'

233
00:10:53,391 --> 00:10:56,394
Just...had stuff to do.

234
00:10:56,481 --> 00:10:58,353
[mouse clicks]

235
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:02,879
[chimes]

236
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:06,230
Hey, take a look at this,
would you?

237
00:11:06,317 --> 00:11:08,885
[groans]

238
00:11:08,972 --> 00:11:12,106
Somebody uploaded a file
onto the major crime server.

239
00:11:12,193 --> 00:11:14,325
The file was transferred
from outside CBI

240
00:11:14,412 --> 00:11:17,851
and that's not possible.
At least it shouldn't be.

241
00:11:17,938 --> 00:11:20,244
That's that "I dare you" thing?

242
00:11:20,331 --> 00:11:22,812
Well, let's take the dare.

243
00:11:22,899 --> 00:11:24,771
[mouse clicks]

244
00:11:27,077 --> 00:11:28,949
[horn honks]

245
00:11:34,258 --> 00:11:37,218
- Isn't that the FBI agent?
- Darcy, yes.

246
00:11:46,314 --> 00:11:48,272
What is going on?

247
00:11:48,359 --> 00:11:49,360
[car door closes]

248
00:11:49,447 --> 00:11:50,710
[alarm chirps]

249
00:11:59,936 --> 00:12:01,416
[lock turns]

250
00:12:01,503 --> 00:12:03,635
[door squeaks]

251
00:12:03,723 --> 00:12:05,246
[door closes]

252
00:12:11,861 --> 00:12:13,428
[chimes]

253
00:12:13,515 --> 00:12:15,473
Somebody just uploaded
a text file

254
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:17,258
to the same folder
as the video.

255
00:12:17,345 --> 00:12:18,520
Open it.

256
00:12:23,177 --> 00:12:24,700
Uh..

257
00:12:24,787 --> 00:12:26,789
Oakview Inn. Oakview Inn.

258
00:12:26,876 --> 00:12:29,400
Quick, let's go.
Let's get outta here. Come on.

259
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:42,370
[engine shuts off]

260
00:12:42,457 --> 00:12:44,328
[[car door closes]

261
00:12:59,604 --> 00:13:01,258
[rattling]

262
00:13:01,345 --> 00:13:03,217
- Grace, Grace, Grace.
- Alright, hold on.

263
00:13:08,396 --> 00:13:09,745
Don't move. Federal agent.

264
00:13:09,832 --> 00:13:11,312
CBI. We're friendly.
Don't shoot.

265
00:13:14,968 --> 00:13:16,621
Patrick?

266
00:13:16,708 --> 00:13:18,667
Susan.

267
00:13:18,754 --> 00:13:20,974
Sorry about your door.

268
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:28,938
[door closes]

269
00:13:33,029 --> 00:13:35,075
Creepy.

270
00:13:35,162 --> 00:13:37,555
Looks like you're my knight in
shining armor, Patrick.

271
00:13:37,642 --> 00:13:39,253
Hmm.

272
00:13:39,340 --> 00:13:42,299
So, I go to CBI
asking about a copycat

273
00:13:42,386 --> 00:13:45,999
and CBI is sent this
weird stalking video of me.

274
00:13:46,086 --> 00:13:48,218
Panzer's killer's taken
an interest in you.

275
00:13:48,305 --> 00:13:51,134
It's dangerous.

276
00:13:51,221 --> 00:13:53,745
I-I think you should
get off the case.

277
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:55,486
You should give it
to someone else.

278
00:13:55,573 --> 00:13:57,097
Come on.

279
00:13:57,184 --> 00:13:59,490
This weird video
makes me wanna

280
00:13:59,577 --> 00:14:01,275
go after after that creep
even harder.

281
00:14:01,362 --> 00:14:04,365
I, uh...this is
a dangerous situation.

282
00:14:04,452 --> 00:14:06,454
We're not talking about
just some peeping tom.

283
00:14:06,541 --> 00:14:08,717
This is a stalker. It's
a totally different pathology.

284
00:14:08,804 --> 00:14:10,893
Goodnight.
I can take care of myself.

285
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:12,982
I appreciate
your help tonight. Thanks.

286
00:14:26,953 --> 00:14:31,218
That video..
It was Red John.

287
00:14:31,305 --> 00:14:33,611
Yes.

288
00:14:35,962 --> 00:14:38,703
Red John
is stalking Darcy.

289
00:14:40,575 --> 00:14:42,925
[sighs]

290
00:14:43,012 --> 00:14:44,361
Yeah.

291
00:14:44,448 --> 00:14:47,712
And you didn't tell her.
Are you crazy?

292
00:14:47,799 --> 00:14:51,064
If I told her, she, she would've
just gone harder at the case.

293
00:14:51,151 --> 00:14:53,066
She's not really
the type to stop.

294
00:14:53,153 --> 00:14:55,807
Mm. Is this
about helping Darcy

295
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:57,461
or keeping Red John
for yourself?

296
00:14:57,548 --> 00:15:01,204
What? This is not about me.
Of course I'm worried about her.

297
00:15:03,598 --> 00:15:07,210
"She's cute.
This is gonna be fun."

298
00:15:07,297 --> 00:15:08,995
That message,
that was meant for you.

299
00:15:09,082 --> 00:15:12,607
Yes, that was
a message for me.

300
00:15:12,694 --> 00:15:15,610
Red John is trying to form
a closer relationship with you.

301
00:15:15,697 --> 00:15:17,177
Yeah, well,
there's no relationship.

302
00:15:17,264 --> 00:15:19,179
What if Red John took
murdering Panzer

303
00:15:19,266 --> 00:15:21,746
as some kind of invitation?

304
00:15:21,833 --> 00:15:23,966
You manipulated
a serial killer.

305
00:15:24,053 --> 00:15:25,011
There's gonna
be consequences.

306
00:15:25,098 --> 00:15:26,708
I didn't have a choice.

307
00:15:26,795 --> 00:15:28,884
I had to stop Panzer.

308
00:15:31,931 --> 00:15:35,412
I will try and get Darcy
off the Panzer case..

309
00:15:35,499 --> 00:15:38,546
...before Red John hurts her.

310
00:15:38,633 --> 00:15:40,156
I hope you can.

311
00:15:47,337 --> 00:15:49,905
You had a fight
with your partner, Alton Creek?

312
00:15:49,992 --> 00:15:51,341
A disagreement, agent.
Not--

313
00:15:51,428 --> 00:15:53,953
I'd like to hear it
from him, Ms. Santori.

314
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:56,564
It was a disagreement.
Not a fight.

315
00:15:56,651 --> 00:15:58,566
About money?

316
00:15:58,653 --> 00:16:02,787
Go ahead.
You can answer.

317
00:16:02,874 --> 00:16:05,225
Yes, we argued over money.

318
00:16:05,312 --> 00:16:07,618
Some of his clients were
behind on their bills

319
00:16:07,705 --> 00:16:10,273
and I told Al
to get on them about it.

320
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:13,059
Look, he was my partner
for 15 years.

321
00:16:13,146 --> 00:16:15,800
I had nothing but love
for the guy.

322
00:16:15,887 --> 00:16:18,107
'This is from the security
camera outside your building'

323
00:16:18,194 --> 00:16:19,761
'the day before Creek died.'

324
00:16:23,504 --> 00:16:25,158
Doesn't look much like love.

325
00:16:25,245 --> 00:16:28,074
What were you really
arguing about?

326
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:29,597
I told you.

327
00:16:29,684 --> 00:16:32,121
Money.

328
00:16:32,208 --> 00:16:34,123
You know who
you should look at

329
00:16:34,210 --> 00:16:35,864
is that hooker
he hung out with.

330
00:16:35,951 --> 00:16:37,344
Summer Edgecombe?

331
00:16:37,431 --> 00:16:38,998
Summer... Autumn,
something like that.

332
00:16:39,085 --> 00:16:40,651
She's got
a terrible temper.

333
00:16:40,738 --> 00:16:42,479
If there's nothing else,
we'll get going--

334
00:16:42,566 --> 00:16:44,916
Just one more thing.
You lied to us yesterday.

335
00:16:45,004 --> 00:16:46,701
We asked if anyone
wanted Alton dead

336
00:16:46,788 --> 00:16:49,182
and you said no,
but wasn't that a lie.

337
00:16:49,269 --> 00:16:51,532
Do you know who sent this
to your partner?

338
00:16:53,664 --> 00:16:55,144
Did you do it?

339
00:17:01,063 --> 00:17:03,674
That is disgusting.

340
00:17:03,761 --> 00:17:06,764
And I certainly
didn't do it.

341
00:17:06,851 --> 00:17:08,331
Are we done here?

342
00:17:12,379 --> 00:17:14,250
- So?
- So I asked around.

343
00:17:14,337 --> 00:17:16,296
"did Dersh happen to mention
Al's little secret?"

344
00:17:16,383 --> 00:17:19,038
and by little, I mean big,
'cause he had to know.

345
00:17:19,125 --> 00:17:20,213
What secret?

346
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:22,171
Cost you $1,000 to find out.

347
00:17:24,608 --> 00:17:26,871
Thousand dollars?

348
00:17:26,958 --> 00:17:29,048
And dinner
at a nice restaurant.

349
00:17:30,527 --> 00:17:33,052
Five hundred and a pizza.

350
00:17:33,139 --> 00:17:34,531
Six hundred, no mushrooms.

351
00:17:34,618 --> 00:17:36,446
Deal.

352
00:17:36,533 --> 00:17:38,405
I hate mushrooms.

353
00:17:41,538 --> 00:17:45,847
What can you get
for $127.68?

354
00:17:45,934 --> 00:17:47,631
You can go crazy
at olive garden.

355
00:17:47,718 --> 00:17:49,155
Not me. Creek.

356
00:17:49,242 --> 00:17:50,939
Van Pelt looked
into his business records

357
00:17:51,026 --> 00:17:52,114
and there's something weird.

358
00:17:52,201 --> 00:17:53,811
Up until six months ago

359
00:17:53,898 --> 00:17:56,901
every week, he put in
for reimbursement by his firm

360
00:17:56,988 --> 00:18:01,036
for $127.68 for expenses.

361
00:18:01,123 --> 00:18:03,560
So he was hiding whatever it is
from his partner.

362
00:18:03,647 --> 00:18:08,261
Yeah, but what can you
buy for $127.68?

363
00:18:08,348 --> 00:18:11,612
I don't know. Creek was
hiding something else.

364
00:18:11,699 --> 00:18:15,006
Our C.I. Found out he had a big
gambling habit. Poker, mostly.

365
00:18:15,094 --> 00:18:16,617
- Illegal?
- Very.

366
00:18:16,704 --> 00:18:18,923
San Francisco has an underground
club in the tenderloin.

367
00:18:19,010 --> 00:18:20,664
Creek had lost plenty lately.

368
00:18:20,751 --> 00:18:22,971
That could have been why
he and Dersh fought.

369
00:18:23,058 --> 00:18:25,539
Yeah, but a few days later,
Creek won 50 grand

370
00:18:25,626 --> 00:18:28,281
at Texas hold'em, which turned
out to be a bigger problem.

371
00:18:28,368 --> 00:18:30,892
- Why?
- He won it from Brock Marx.

372
00:18:30,979 --> 00:18:32,633
Bay area crime lord.

373
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:35,070
Marx accused Creek of cheating,
threatened to kill him.

374
00:18:35,157 --> 00:18:36,941
Felony narcotics, assault

375
00:18:37,028 --> 00:18:38,726
assault with a deadly,
and attempted murder.

376
00:18:38,813 --> 00:18:41,250
A mobbed-up guy like this
could leave a pig's head

377
00:18:41,337 --> 00:18:43,078
outside somebody's door.
Go pick him up.

378
00:18:43,165 --> 00:18:46,168
He's in the wind. Parole officer
hasn't heard from him in weeks.

379
00:18:46,255 --> 00:18:47,604
Crap.

380
00:18:47,691 --> 00:18:50,172
We think we can find him
at this gambling club

381
00:18:50,259 --> 00:18:52,392
but SFPD Is busting
the place next week.

382
00:18:52,479 --> 00:18:54,742
- Asked us to go in quiet.
- How quiet?

383
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:00,313
[techno music]

384
00:19:30,430 --> 00:19:32,258
Hey.

385
00:19:32,345 --> 00:19:34,216
There's Marx.

386
00:19:47,403 --> 00:19:48,796
You okay.

387
00:19:48,883 --> 00:19:50,058
You no.

388
00:19:51,799 --> 00:19:54,193
I wanna play craps.
This is the only table.

389
00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:57,892
Too bad.

390
00:19:57,979 --> 00:20:01,156
It's okay, baby.
I'll play for the both of us.

391
00:20:01,243 --> 00:20:03,941
I bet I do better than you would
have done anyways.

392
00:20:04,028 --> 00:20:06,335
Go get a drink.
This won't take long.

393
00:20:06,422 --> 00:20:09,599
[man] $63.

394
00:20:09,686 --> 00:20:11,035
Hi.

395
00:20:13,386 --> 00:20:16,084
[indistinct conversations]

396
00:20:31,665 --> 00:20:33,493
What is it?
What happened?

397
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:36,322
Darcy's okay.
It's not her.

398
00:20:36,409 --> 00:20:39,020
Not directly anyway.

399
00:20:39,107 --> 00:20:41,979
Panzer..
The gift that keeps on giving.

400
00:20:45,156 --> 00:20:46,810
Do you remember Tom Maier?

401
00:20:46,897 --> 00:20:49,422
He was the father
of Panzer's first victim?

402
00:20:49,509 --> 00:20:52,251
Sure. Nice man.

403
00:20:54,818 --> 00:20:56,516
He killed himself today.

404
00:20:58,866 --> 00:21:00,259
Oh.

405
00:21:08,615 --> 00:21:10,138
Oh.

406
00:21:19,016 --> 00:21:20,191
[speaks indistinctly]

407
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:22,019
[sighs]
Oh, well.

408
00:21:24,283 --> 00:21:25,849
[chips rattling]

409
00:21:25,936 --> 00:21:27,764
Try again.

410
00:21:27,851 --> 00:21:29,592
Thanks.

411
00:21:29,679 --> 00:21:31,638
Side bet?

412
00:21:31,725 --> 00:21:33,553
Like?

413
00:21:37,383 --> 00:21:38,949
[whispering indistinctly]

414
00:21:43,824 --> 00:21:45,565
You have a bet.

415
00:21:45,652 --> 00:21:46,957
[chuckles]

416
00:21:54,791 --> 00:21:56,358
'Eight the hard way.
Winner, Eight.'

417
00:21:56,445 --> 00:21:58,055
Ohh.

418
00:22:01,755 --> 00:22:03,583
[exhales deeply]

419
00:22:03,670 --> 00:22:06,760
'And the shooter passes. We got
a new shooter coming out?'

420
00:22:09,502 --> 00:22:11,417
Pay up.

421
00:22:11,504 --> 00:22:12,635
Right now?

422
00:22:12,722 --> 00:22:13,854
Why not?

423
00:22:13,941 --> 00:22:15,334
Here?

424
00:22:45,799 --> 00:22:48,454
So you're pretty much
tall, dark, and handsome.

425
00:22:48,541 --> 00:22:51,500
- Yeah.
- I like that.

426
00:22:51,587 --> 00:22:56,200
Police. Brock Marx.
You're under arrest.

427
00:22:56,287 --> 00:22:57,288
[handcuffs clicking]

428
00:22:57,376 --> 00:22:58,464
For what?

429
00:22:58,551 --> 00:23:00,291
Parole violation.

430
00:23:00,379 --> 00:23:03,207
We need to ask you a few
questions about a murder.

431
00:23:03,294 --> 00:23:05,645
[exhales deeply]

432
00:23:05,732 --> 00:23:06,950
Nice work.

433
00:23:07,037 --> 00:23:08,430
Thank you.

434
00:23:10,084 --> 00:23:12,739
What'd you whisper
to him?

435
00:23:12,826 --> 00:23:15,045
Wouldn't you like to know?

436
00:23:36,502 --> 00:23:39,418
[dramatic music]

437
00:23:51,168 --> 00:23:52,343
'Morning, Lisbon.'

438
00:23:52,431 --> 00:23:54,171
Uh, I need to borrow
your office.

439
00:23:54,258 --> 00:23:58,741
Too bad. I'm busy.
What costs $127.68?

440
00:23:58,828 --> 00:24:02,353
Creek spent that much every week
for months and was hiding it.

441
00:24:02,441 --> 00:24:05,095
Until three months ago. Hmm.
Anyway, your office?

442
00:24:05,182 --> 00:24:07,141
Are you gonna actually
help with this case?

443
00:24:07,228 --> 00:24:10,623
Well, as soon as I can get
Darcy off Panzer's case.

444
00:24:10,710 --> 00:24:12,233
Mr. Jane.

445
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:14,540
Mrs. Maier, please,
thanks for coming to our office.

446
00:24:14,627 --> 00:24:17,934
Have a seat. Lisbon, this is
Teri Maier, Tom Maier's wife.

447
00:24:18,021 --> 00:24:22,765
Mrs. Maier, we are
so sorry for your loss.

448
00:24:22,852 --> 00:24:26,160
The gardener found him
in the backyard.

449
00:24:26,247 --> 00:24:28,423
Tom made sure it wasn't me
who found him.

450
00:24:28,510 --> 00:24:29,990
'Yeah.'

451
00:24:31,905 --> 00:24:34,429
The coroner's office
hasn't released the body yet.

452
00:24:34,516 --> 00:24:36,649
I need to bury him.

453
00:24:36,736 --> 00:24:39,782
Of course.
I'll check it out immediately.

454
00:24:39,869 --> 00:24:41,131
Thank you.

455
00:24:41,218 --> 00:24:44,874
Okay. I'm so sorry
for what happened.

456
00:24:44,961 --> 00:24:46,702
It was the guilt.

457
00:24:46,789 --> 00:24:48,661
It ate him up like a cancer.

458
00:24:50,184 --> 00:24:52,795
Every day Tom got smaller
and smaller

459
00:24:52,882 --> 00:24:54,405
until finally he left.

460
00:24:54,493 --> 00:24:56,582
Did he happen to
leave a note?

461
00:24:56,669 --> 00:24:58,497
No.

462
00:24:58,584 --> 00:25:00,499
But honestly, I haven't checked.

463
00:25:01,978 --> 00:25:03,676
He felt bad.

464
00:25:03,763 --> 00:25:07,027
So the only thing he could do
was hang himself.

465
00:25:10,247 --> 00:25:13,424
He was in so much pain,
and all I feel is angry.

466
00:25:13,512 --> 00:25:15,296
We will do everything
we can

467
00:25:15,383 --> 00:25:18,560
to get the coroner to release
the body as soon as possible.

468
00:25:22,390 --> 00:25:23,870
Okay. Thank you.

469
00:25:31,486 --> 00:25:34,445
Uh, I got a lot to do,
so have a nice day.

470
00:25:34,533 --> 00:25:36,752
- Jane?
- Yes.

471
00:25:36,839 --> 00:25:38,537
Is there something
you wanna tell me?

472
00:25:38,624 --> 00:25:41,496
Deniability, Lisbon.
Deniability. Your best friend.

473
00:25:56,859 --> 00:25:58,687
'Sent to Creek
before he was killed.'

474
00:26:00,559 --> 00:26:02,822
A warning of what's
to come, huh?

475
00:26:02,909 --> 00:26:05,955
Make him afraid, paranoid?

476
00:26:06,042 --> 00:26:07,653
This is just your style..

477
00:26:07,740 --> 00:26:09,480
...cheap, gruesome, sadistic.

478
00:26:16,096 --> 00:26:18,489
You got a question for me?

479
00:26:18,577 --> 00:26:20,579
Or you just wanna chat?

480
00:26:23,016 --> 00:26:24,539
You can keep that.

481
00:26:26,628 --> 00:26:29,979
Lisbon, it's Jane. Let's have
lunch in San Francisco.

482
00:26:30,066 --> 00:26:33,374
What do you say? Call me.

483
00:26:45,081 --> 00:26:48,171
Teresa, aw, can't tell you
how great it is to see you.

484
00:26:49,869 --> 00:26:51,566
Partnered with this one
for three years.

485
00:26:51,653 --> 00:26:53,612
Oh, I know. Patrick Jane.

486
00:26:53,699 --> 00:26:55,352
Bobby Wilson.

487
00:26:55,439 --> 00:26:57,006
So how's it goin'?

488
00:26:57,093 --> 00:26:59,182
How's the big old CBI?

489
00:26:59,269 --> 00:27:01,924
Oh, you know, it goes.

490
00:27:02,011 --> 00:27:04,666
FBI's all over us
about this, uh, Panzer case.

491
00:27:04,753 --> 00:27:08,235
Oh, feebs. They were jackbooting
about it over here, too.

492
00:27:08,322 --> 00:27:09,976
Anyway, what
do you say, Bobby?

493
00:27:10,063 --> 00:27:12,805
Could you show us your, uh,
file on the Panzer case?

494
00:27:12,892 --> 00:27:15,242
Uh, yeah, sure.

495
00:27:15,329 --> 00:27:19,289
Just have CBI file a request.
It's form 29.

496
00:27:19,376 --> 00:27:20,856
Oh, of course.
Yeah. Yeah.

497
00:27:20,943 --> 00:27:23,032
We were hoping that
maybe we could, uh..

498
00:27:23,119 --> 00:27:25,992
...expedite the process,
you know, talk to you.

499
00:27:26,079 --> 00:27:28,037
We're under the gun
with time.

500
00:27:30,257 --> 00:27:32,128
He's okay, Bobby.

501
00:27:38,874 --> 00:27:41,050
Did you find any..
You know, never mind.

502
00:27:41,137 --> 00:27:43,270
Deniability.

503
00:27:43,357 --> 00:27:45,968
Guess what we found out?

504
00:27:46,055 --> 00:27:48,449
California bar was investigating
your law partner

505
00:27:48,536 --> 00:27:50,190
over his gambling.

506
00:27:52,322 --> 00:27:54,847
That's what your fight was
about last week, wasn't it?

507
00:27:58,198 --> 00:28:00,679
Yes. Alton was
out of control.

508
00:28:00,766 --> 00:28:03,116
- So you lied to us.
- Don't answer that.

509
00:28:03,203 --> 00:28:05,858
If al got disbarred,
that was the end of your firm.

510
00:28:05,945 --> 00:28:07,424
He'd become a liability.

511
00:28:07,511 --> 00:28:10,601
And how do lawyers
deal with liability?

512
00:28:10,689 --> 00:28:13,039
Oh, don't worry.
It's not a lawyer joke.

513
00:28:13,126 --> 00:28:15,084
[Van Pelt]
'They get insurance.'

514
00:28:15,171 --> 00:28:17,652
You insured Alton Creek's life
for $10 million.

515
00:28:17,739 --> 00:28:19,262
'Four weeks ago.'

516
00:28:20,699 --> 00:28:22,831
Larry?

517
00:28:22,918 --> 00:28:25,051
I didn't have time
to mention it.

518
00:28:25,138 --> 00:28:27,575
It's common for businesses
to have a "key man" policy.

519
00:28:27,662 --> 00:28:29,185
Al was "key."

520
00:28:29,272 --> 00:28:31,187
Uh, you know what?
We're done here.

521
00:28:31,274 --> 00:28:34,582
Admit it. Alton was worth more
to you dead so you killed him.

522
00:28:34,669 --> 00:28:36,062
- I said we're done.
- Fine.

523
00:28:36,149 --> 00:28:37,454
Let's go, Mr. Dersh.

524
00:28:39,761 --> 00:28:44,853
Agent, seriously, I don't think
Larry is capable of this.

525
00:28:44,940 --> 00:28:48,770
Don't be naive.
Men lie...and kill.

526
00:28:55,124 --> 00:28:56,430
Maier?

527
00:28:56,517 --> 00:28:58,345
Yes. Thomas.

528
00:28:58,432 --> 00:28:59,999
Right in here.

529
00:29:14,143 --> 00:29:15,623
Five minutes.

530
00:29:15,710 --> 00:29:17,190
Thank you.

531
00:30:07,196 --> 00:30:09,590
Hey, we've had Brock Marx
in the box for hours.

532
00:30:09,677 --> 00:30:11,157
The guy won't talk.

533
00:30:11,244 --> 00:30:14,900
Ah. Theory number one..
Crime lord. Pig's head. Yes.

534
00:30:14,987 --> 00:30:16,727
We've got his law partner
as well.

535
00:30:16,815 --> 00:30:18,512
Insurance motive.
You want to question him?

536
00:30:18,599 --> 00:30:20,340
Kind of busy right now.

537
00:30:20,427 --> 00:30:22,385
Why don't you guys
get your suspects together?

538
00:30:22,472 --> 00:30:25,040
I'll meet you back here
in some time. I promise.

539
00:30:25,127 --> 00:30:27,347
- Where are you going?
- Got a find a hobo.

540
00:30:27,434 --> 00:30:30,567
Do some errands. Then I gotta
run by this flower shop, too.

541
00:30:30,654 --> 00:30:32,439
What? Find a hobo?

542
00:30:32,526 --> 00:30:34,267
Flower shop.

543
00:30:43,232 --> 00:30:45,539
[knock on door]

544
00:30:53,025 --> 00:30:55,897
[clacking]

545
00:30:58,378 --> 00:31:03,470
[cell phone rings]

546
00:31:03,557 --> 00:31:06,690
[ring]

547
00:31:06,777 --> 00:31:08,518
Patrick, it's Susan.

548
00:31:08,605 --> 00:31:10,956
Ah, Susan.
What do I owe the pleasure?

549
00:31:11,043 --> 00:31:12,522
'Well, I'm callingyouback.'

550
00:31:12,609 --> 00:31:14,220
Oh, that's right. Yeah.

551
00:31:14,307 --> 00:31:17,266
'I wanted to let you know
I went down to the SFPD.'

552
00:31:17,353 --> 00:31:18,615
'I didn't find anything.'

553
00:31:18,702 --> 00:31:20,182
'I know.
So, why'd you go?'

554
00:31:20,269 --> 00:31:22,402
Well, this Panzer-Red John
copycat thing

555
00:31:22,489 --> 00:31:24,404
has me very intrigued.

556
00:31:24,491 --> 00:31:28,277
'I wanna help out as much
as I possibly can.'

557
00:31:28,364 --> 00:31:31,585
So, uh..
Susan, you still there?

558
00:31:31,672 --> 00:31:34,457
Listen, I need to talk to you
about a couple things.

559
00:31:34,544 --> 00:31:36,155
- I've gotta go.
- Wait. Patrick?

560
00:31:36,242 --> 00:31:37,939
[door squeaks]

561
00:31:39,071 --> 00:31:40,028
[door closes]

562
00:31:40,115 --> 00:31:41,421
'Patrick, are you there?'

563
00:31:41,508 --> 00:31:43,162
[whispers]
'Bye.'

564
00:31:44,815 --> 00:31:46,382
[keys clatter]

565
00:31:56,653 --> 00:31:58,220
'Hello?'

566
00:32:03,617 --> 00:32:05,880
[door closes]

567
00:32:14,236 --> 00:32:15,455
[elevator bell dings]

568
00:32:15,542 --> 00:32:17,065
- Patrick.
- Susan.

569
00:32:17,152 --> 00:32:18,849
I was hoping we could
talk some--

570
00:32:18,937 --> 00:32:20,242
Jane, they're waiting
for you.

571
00:32:20,329 --> 00:32:22,549
Hang on one second.
I have a few questions--

572
00:32:22,636 --> 00:32:25,160
You're more than welcome to wait
in the interview room

573
00:32:25,247 --> 00:32:27,684
but this one hasn't done squat
on our murder investigation

574
00:32:27,771 --> 00:32:29,208
and he's damn well
gonna help.

575
00:32:29,295 --> 00:32:30,992
Yes, I damn well am gonna help.

576
00:32:31,079 --> 00:32:32,951
I'm sorry.
I won't be long.

577
00:32:34,822 --> 00:32:36,171
Thanks for rescuing me.

578
00:32:36,258 --> 00:32:38,608
Shh!

579
00:32:38,695 --> 00:32:40,393
- Hey.
- Hey.

580
00:32:40,480 --> 00:32:41,698
I didn't call you in.

581
00:32:41,785 --> 00:32:43,744
Oh. Mr. Jane asked me
to come in.

582
00:32:46,399 --> 00:32:48,836
Hi. Okay, folks.
Let's get straight to it.

583
00:32:48,923 --> 00:32:51,360
Patrick Jane.
Nice to meet you.

584
00:32:51,447 --> 00:32:53,797
Do you prefer eggs
for breakfast over cereal?

585
00:32:53,884 --> 00:32:55,538
Cereal.

586
00:32:55,625 --> 00:32:58,367
Hardened criminal like yourself.
That's very interesting.

587
00:32:58,454 --> 00:33:00,848
What's your favorite
legal objection?

588
00:33:00,935 --> 00:33:04,634
Better to be feared or loved?
You don't have to answer that.

589
00:33:06,288 --> 00:33:08,160
'Summer.'

590
00:33:08,247 --> 00:33:10,336
Patrick Jane.
Pleasure to finally meet you.

591
00:33:10,423 --> 00:33:11,380
Hi.

592
00:33:11,467 --> 00:33:12,947
You..

593
00:33:13,034 --> 00:33:15,515
'You are
a borderline sociopath.'

594
00:33:15,602 --> 00:33:17,169
Extremely violent.

595
00:33:17,256 --> 00:33:19,519
But not our killer.

596
00:33:19,606 --> 00:33:21,651
You were jealous
of the victim's talent

597
00:33:21,738 --> 00:33:23,131
his skills
in the courtroom

598
00:33:23,218 --> 00:33:25,003
but he never, uh,
bought you flowers.

599
00:33:25,090 --> 00:33:26,482
Flowers? No.

600
00:33:26,569 --> 00:33:28,832
Yeah. The flower shop
nearest your office

601
00:33:28,919 --> 00:33:31,052
uh, sells a dozen red roses

602
00:33:31,139 --> 00:33:35,839
for a very reasonable price
of $127.68.

603
00:33:35,926 --> 00:33:37,928
It was flowers?
Seriously?

604
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:41,193
Yes. Our romantic victim
bought them once a week.

605
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:43,760
A secret relationship
he couldn't disclose..

606
00:33:43,847 --> 00:33:45,501
An illicit one.

607
00:33:47,634 --> 00:33:49,288
Maybe not that illicit.

608
00:33:49,375 --> 00:33:52,465
No offense. But how many men
do buy roses for hookers?

609
00:33:52,552 --> 00:33:54,989
Oh. I don't hook anymore.

610
00:33:55,076 --> 00:33:57,905
But they do buy them
for girlfriends.

611
00:33:57,992 --> 00:34:01,822
Girlfriends they can't have
under certain rules.

612
00:34:01,909 --> 00:34:03,606
You killed Alton Creek.

613
00:34:03,693 --> 00:34:06,827
[scoffs]
Uh, that's ridiculous.

614
00:34:06,914 --> 00:34:08,959
I'm an officer of the court.

615
00:34:10,874 --> 00:34:13,225
I see homicidal rage,
betrayal.

616
00:34:13,312 --> 00:34:14,922
You were lovers.
Yes, that's right.

617
00:34:15,009 --> 00:34:17,968
And he dumped you.
Hence, the pig's head.

618
00:34:19,187 --> 00:34:20,928
Pig for a pig.

619
00:34:21,015 --> 00:34:23,452
Ms. Santori, we'll have
to ask you a few questions--

620
00:34:23,539 --> 00:34:25,846
You killed Al?
You bitch!

621
00:34:25,933 --> 00:34:27,108
[Patrick]
Oh. Hello.

622
00:34:28,805 --> 00:34:31,721
[grunting]

623
00:34:33,506 --> 00:34:34,811
Have a nice day.

624
00:34:34,898 --> 00:34:36,552
Ugh!

625
00:34:39,338 --> 00:34:42,341
Al was an okay guy.
Why the hell did you kill him?

626
00:34:42,428 --> 00:34:44,821
- I got this, Summer.
- "An okay guy"?

627
00:34:44,908 --> 00:34:47,476
He said he'd marry me,
the bastard!

628
00:34:47,563 --> 00:34:49,174
And then he changed his mind.

629
00:34:49,261 --> 00:34:51,524
He just wasn't ready,
wasn't sure of his feelings.

630
00:34:51,611 --> 00:34:54,353
Oh, a divorce attorney who was
holding out for true love..

631
00:34:54,440 --> 00:34:57,138
The affair ended months ago,
long time to hold a grudge.

632
00:34:57,225 --> 00:34:58,966
Can you take her
to holding, please?

633
00:34:59,053 --> 00:35:01,621
Hey, I waited for him
foryears.

634
00:35:01,708 --> 00:35:04,276
I put my career on hold
to be with him.

635
00:35:04,363 --> 00:35:07,148
I wanted a family,
and now it is too late.

636
00:35:07,235 --> 00:35:09,846
He took away my life!

637
00:35:09,933 --> 00:35:12,545
And turns out,
all he wanted was a hooker.

638
00:35:12,632 --> 00:35:14,503
I'm not a hooker, bitch.

639
00:35:16,201 --> 00:35:17,724
Susan, did you wanna
talk now?

640
00:35:17,811 --> 00:35:19,378
I don't have any time.

641
00:35:19,465 --> 00:35:21,902
Field office just got a tip
on the Panzer murder.

642
00:35:21,989 --> 00:35:24,948
Homeless guy found a bag
of stuff in an alley in Fresno.

643
00:35:25,035 --> 00:35:26,428
Tip from a hobo?

644
00:35:26,515 --> 00:35:28,082
My people are on it.

645
00:35:28,169 --> 00:35:31,216
A bag of clothes with blood
spatter on it and a knife.

646
00:35:31,303 --> 00:35:33,740
Looks like it could be
the knife that killed Panzer.

647
00:35:33,827 --> 00:35:37,657
Mm. Copycat's sloppy
if it's his knife.

648
00:35:37,744 --> 00:35:40,007
Red John would never
have been that careless.

649
00:35:40,094 --> 00:35:42,183
Forensics is running
the blood now.

650
00:35:42,270 --> 00:35:45,360
Oh, get this.

651
00:35:45,447 --> 00:35:49,059
The alley is in the neighborhood
near the Maier home.

652
00:35:49,147 --> 00:35:51,671
Thomas Maier? No.

653
00:35:51,758 --> 00:35:54,587
- Yeah. The San Joaquin killer.
- I know who he is.

654
00:35:54,674 --> 00:35:56,371
He's the father
of the first victim.

655
00:35:56,458 --> 00:35:57,981
I-I-I don't buy that.

656
00:35:58,068 --> 00:36:00,158
Maier killed himself
yesterday.

657
00:36:02,029 --> 00:36:04,205
We're going to the house now
to search it.

658
00:36:04,292 --> 00:36:06,033
Okay. Good luck.

659
00:36:20,700 --> 00:36:22,571
I have a confession to make.

660
00:36:24,704 --> 00:36:26,619
When Tom killed himself

661
00:36:26,706 --> 00:36:29,665
'part of me was angry at him
for being weak.'

662
00:36:32,712 --> 00:36:35,628
But now I know how strong
he was.

663
00:36:35,715 --> 00:36:37,717
Now I know.

664
00:36:37,804 --> 00:36:40,241
The FBI found this letter
in molly's room.

665
00:36:40,328 --> 00:36:42,896
It's from Tom.

666
00:36:42,983 --> 00:36:45,681
It's important that you all
hear this

667
00:36:45,768 --> 00:36:47,640
so you can understand.

668
00:36:50,947 --> 00:36:52,558
"my dearest Teri..

669
00:36:54,299 --> 00:36:56,083
"if you are reading this

670
00:36:56,170 --> 00:36:59,434
"you know what I have done.

671
00:36:59,521 --> 00:37:02,132
"I have avenged myself
against Jim Panzer.

672
00:37:03,873 --> 00:37:06,746
"Jim was the man who killed
our molly.

673
00:37:09,531 --> 00:37:12,621
"When I saw him on TV
that night, I knew.

674
00:37:12,708 --> 00:37:16,059
"I knew he'd killed her.

675
00:37:16,146 --> 00:37:21,064
"I can't explain how..
except to say

676
00:37:21,151 --> 00:37:24,198
that molly's heart cried out
to me, and I knew."

677
00:37:26,809 --> 00:37:29,725
[sobbing]

678
00:37:32,206 --> 00:37:33,599
Please.

679
00:37:41,389 --> 00:37:45,306
"Killing the man who murdered
our daughter

680
00:37:45,393 --> 00:37:47,439
"is the best thing
I will ever do.

681
00:37:51,530 --> 00:37:53,096
"I'm at peace.

682
00:37:56,317 --> 00:37:58,276
"I'm just sorry
that I'm leaving you alone

683
00:37:58,363 --> 00:38:01,191
"and I hope you forgive me

684
00:38:01,279 --> 00:38:04,238
"and that in some way,
this letter brings you..

685
00:38:05,979 --> 00:38:07,720
...some kind of comfort."

686
00:38:07,807 --> 00:38:10,113
[paper rustles]

687
00:38:13,073 --> 00:38:14,727
"Love, Tom."

688
00:38:23,126 --> 00:38:27,522
[man]
"Lord, make me
an instrument of your peace.

689
00:38:27,609 --> 00:38:32,222
"where there is hatred,
let me so love.

690
00:38:32,310 --> 00:38:36,096
And where there is darkness,
light. Amen."

691
00:38:47,325 --> 00:38:49,762
That's very nice of you
to give her that letter.

692
00:38:49,849 --> 00:38:51,851
Oh, well, it's the least
I could do.

693
00:38:51,938 --> 00:38:53,766
My god, poor woman.

694
00:38:53,853 --> 00:38:56,334
Yeah.

695
00:38:56,421 --> 00:38:58,858
So...the case is closed.

696
00:38:58,945 --> 00:39:01,774
Once we got the labs back,
it was a slam dunk.

697
00:39:01,861 --> 00:39:05,168
'The prints
on the knife, Maier's.'

698
00:39:11,914 --> 00:39:13,786
'The blood was Panzer's.
It was denatured'

699
00:39:13,873 --> 00:39:15,744
'but it was definitely his.'

700
00:39:25,841 --> 00:39:28,061
But why Red John? Why..

701
00:39:28,148 --> 00:39:30,106
Why would he copycat
a dead man?

702
00:39:30,193 --> 00:39:32,979
I don't know.

703
00:39:33,066 --> 00:39:36,286
Maybe he felt like he had to
emulate a monster to hunt one.

704
00:39:36,374 --> 00:39:40,116
So he picked the biggest one
he could find.

705
00:39:40,203 --> 00:39:41,901
One thing I can't
figure out, though

706
00:39:41,988 --> 00:39:44,730
is why he was stalking me,
and why the video?

707
00:39:46,819 --> 00:39:48,864
Hunting monsters
changes you.

708
00:39:50,692 --> 00:39:52,607
Went and killed himself
the very next day.

709
00:39:52,694 --> 00:39:56,437
I hope he found peace
in the end.

710
00:39:56,524 --> 00:39:58,483
Goodbye, agent Darcy.

711
00:40:05,272 --> 00:40:07,361
Did it change you, Patrick?

712
00:40:08,928 --> 00:40:10,320
Hunting a monster?

713
00:40:11,974 --> 00:40:13,411
Of course.

714
00:40:15,674 --> 00:40:17,545
Be well, Susan.

715
00:40:23,638 --> 00:40:25,901
Ithaschanged you,
you know.

716
00:40:28,077 --> 00:40:30,340
Darcy's off the case, Lisbon.
She's safe.

717
00:40:30,428 --> 00:40:33,300
And what about you?
How is Red John gonna feel

718
00:40:33,387 --> 00:40:36,259
when he finds out you spoiled
his fun with agent Darcy?

